    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='Server-Side Request Forgery with Spring Boot',
         body=None,
         narration='Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains S S R F with Spring Boot, in Java. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] When a Spring Boot shop fetches an image from a customer\'s address, the server\'s network position becomes the attacker\'s; a name blocklist is read one way by the check and another by the network, so the fix is to resolve the name once, judge every resulting address, connect to the address that was judged, revalidate each redirect, and limit the type, size and time of the answer. [[slnc 600]] Think of it this way. [[slnc 300]] A receptionist who will phone any number a visitor writes on a slip. The visitor cannot enter the back office, but the receptionist\'s phone can reach every extension. If the receptionist checks the slip for the word "payroll" and the visitor writes the extension number instead, the call goes through. [[slnc 700]] In this video, the domain is an online store\'s product image import. [[slnc 500]] By the end, you will be able to: [[slnc 300]] Explain why a server that fetches a customer\'s address is reachable from outside. [[slnc 300]] Show why a blocklist of names fails against the resolver. [[slnc 300]] Judge every resolved address and connect to the judged address.'),
